WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:06.790
Now, when working with alpha information inside of Unreal Engine 5, there are two ways to

2
00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:08.050
go about this.

3
00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:11.750
The first way is to use an embedded alpha, and an example of this is on the left-hand

4
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.169
side of the screen.

5
00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:18.149
The embedded alpha works by storing the alpha information in the alpha channel of

6
00:00:18.149 --> 00:00:24.420
any texture, and then we sample that information by pulling from the alpha output of our

7
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.100
texture sampler and wiring that into our opacity mask, and while this is a valid

8
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:33.820
way to work, the issue is that any information that's stored inside of the alpha channel

9
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:37.179
of a texture comes into Unreal uncompressed.

10
00:00:37.179 --> 00:00:41.659
This makes that texture take up roughly two times the amount of memory.

11
00:00:41.659 --> 00:00:45.020
Now on the right-hand side of the screen, we have the same exact texture, but this

12
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:51.439
time we have separated the alpha channel out into its own texture, and working like

13
00:00:51.439 --> 00:00:56.560
another material sampler inside of our material, we simply feed that information into our

14
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.020
opacity mask.

15
00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:05.819
This has the added advantage of allowing us to have a cheaper texture footprint, and

16
00:01:05.819 --> 00:01:11.870
also allows us to do something interesting, which we're going to take a look at in this slide.

17
00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:18.069
Now here we have an embedded versus a separate alpha for a leaf texture, and in the slide

18
00:01:18.069 --> 00:01:21.790
in front of us, everything looks kind of similar, but let's go ahead and take a look

19
00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:27.549
at this inside of Unreal to see what the advantage and disadvantage of these methods are.

20
00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:31.590
So inside of Unreal, here is our scene, and if we look at these in close inspection, they

21
00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:37.150
look pretty good, they're looking very similar to one another, so let's say that this one

22
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.430
over here, which is our embedded, let's open up its material so we can see it's

23
00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:45.609
just got a simple material setup, but say a technical director or a technical artist,

24
00:01:45.609 --> 00:01:49.870
somebody comes by and says, hey, we need to get back some memory from our alpha.

25
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:53.430
So what we can actually do with this is if we open up this texture here, if we come

26
00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:59.950
to our LOD bias, we can quickly just bias this down, and that is going to reduce this in size.

27
00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:05.230
Now if we take a look at this, and let's just compare by setting this back to zero,

28
00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:09.710
so we can see here that while it does take it down in size, it does reduce the

29
00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:11.229
quality of this a little bit.

30
00:02:11.229 --> 00:02:17.469
So inside of the branch here, we're losing a lot of the detail, it looks a little just

31
00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:21.229
kind of muddy versus setting this at zero here.

32
00:02:21.229 --> 00:02:24.189
So let's go ahead and leave that at one, let's just close this.

33
00:02:24.189 --> 00:02:30.479
So over here, what we have is again the exact same setup, but slightly different in

34
00:02:30.479 --> 00:02:34.719
that it uses the alpha channel as a separate texture.

35
00:02:34.719 --> 00:02:38.800
So what we can do actually here is, check this out, we can open up this alpha, and

36
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:45.639
if we come here and change our LOD bias to one, we've set this down to 85 KB in size,

37
00:02:45.639 --> 00:02:51.639
and if we take a look at that, we've got some little dots there, but we can

38
00:02:51.639 --> 00:02:55.240
try and fix that if we come here to our MIP gen settings and set like a

39
00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.039
sharpen filter, look at that.

40
00:02:57.039 --> 00:02:58.680
So we sharpened that back up.

41
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:05.280
So what we have done is we have actually improved this by reducing it.

42
00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.039
We've made it take up less texture memory while still maintaining the exact

43
00:03:10.039 --> 00:03:11.960
same visual results.

44
00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.000
And this is really important because all of our optimizations should allow us to

45
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.599
still have the artwork look the way the artist intended it.

46
00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:23.439
So we always want to try to make sure that when we're optimizing stuff, we're not destroying

47
00:03:23.439 --> 00:03:24.759
the artwork.

48
00:03:24.759 --> 00:03:31.000
And this is a great example of how we can allow artists freedom, but also have the

49
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.319
freedom to make some performance optimizations should we need to based on content demands.
